INT. DISUSED SWIMMING POOL, DRAINED - DAWN

A concrete bowl, cracked and stained, ringed with faded lane markers. Pigeons nest in the diving platform. Light slants through a skylight clouded with grime.

TEODORA "TEDDY" WRZESIEN (17, wiry, a swimmer's shoulders, a hoodie that says CITY NATATORIUM) stands at the deep end in bare feet, a jump rope coiled around her wrist. Across the pool, her coach MERCEDES ARRIAGA-KOWAL (50s, whistle, a face carved from patience) sits on a folding chair at the shallow end, a stopwatch resting on her knee.

MERCEDES
Come on, Teddy. Twenty more.

TEDDY
It's drained, Coach. There's no water.

MERCEDES
There's no water, and you still have to move.

TEDDY
Why?

MERCEDES
Because the meet is Saturday and you don't have a pool.

Teddy loops the rope over her head and begins to skip, the soles slapping the bare concrete. Each landing echoes. The pigeons shift.

MERCEDES (CONT'D)
Knees, Teddy. Higher.

TEDDY
They're at my chin.

MERCEDES
Then your chin goes higher.

Teddy laughs, losing the rhythm. The rope snags. She curses. A figure appears at the pool's edge, backlit: DESMOND "DEZ" ADEWALE-FRIEDMAN (18, letterman jacket, a duffel bag, a scowl). He descends the pool ladder, rung by rung, and drops onto the floor.

DEZ
You're skipping rope in an empty pool.

TEDDY
You're trespassing.

DEZ
I'm your ride.

TEDDY
I didn't ask for a ride.

DEZ
Mercedes did.

Teddy looks across the pool. Mercedes shrugs, not guilty in the slightest.

MERCEDES
Dez drove up from the state academy. He wants to race you.

TEDDY
Race me where?

DEZ
In there.

He nods at the pool. Teddy follows his gaze to the dark, cracked bowl.

TEDDY
It's dry.

DEZ
So we run it. Deep end to shallow. Whoever touches the wall first wins.

TEDDY
That's stupid.

DEZ
Scared?

TEDDY
Of a boy who can't swim a mile?

DEZ
I swam a mile Tuesday.

TEDDY
In a lake. With a wetsuit.

DEZ
I'm standing here, aren't I?

MERCEDES
Gentlemen. Lady. Take your marks.

Teddy tosses the rope. Dez drops his duffel. They crouch at the deep end, shoulder to shoulder. Neither looks at the other. The skylight light brightens a fraction.

MERCEDES (CONT'D)
Set.

TEDDY
(low)
You're going to lose.

DEZ
(low)
I know.

MERCEDES
Go.

They launch. Feet slap concrete. Two shapes sprint up the long slope of the pool floor, breath ragged, echoes ricocheting off the tile. For a moment, as the sun hits them, the whole bowl seems to fill with water.
